      Enhancing landslide susceptibility mapping in the Himalayas: geospatial and machine learning with explainable AI

        Gondwana Research
         · Utthasini, M., Ilampooranan, I., Singh, S. K., Kanga, S., Kumar, P., Halder, K., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Landslides are a frequent and deadly hazard in Uttarakhand, a mountainous state in the Indian Himalayas. This study maps where landslides are most likely to occur and explains &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; those places are at risk.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Adding a wide range of geospatial datasets clearly improved the models' ability to identify landslide-prone areas.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Explainable AI showed which terrain and environmental factors contribute most to slope failure.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;Ensemble machine learning models were trained on 35 geo-environmental variables. SHAP, an explainable-AI technique, was used to show how much each factor contributes to the predictions.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;Maps that are both accurate and explainable help planners and disaster managers trust the results and target hazard reduction in the Himalayas.&lt;/p&gt;







