      A robust and scalable crop mapping framework using advanced machine learning and optical and SAR imageries

        Smart Agricultural Technology
         · Halder, K., Srivastava, A. K., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Planning for food security and sustainable farming needs accurate maps of which crop grows where, every year. We built a reproducible method that makes these maps from satellite images and AI.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;The best model, a BiLSTM (a type of deep learning model for sequences), reached about 93% overall accuracy.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Combining radar and optical satellite data improved accuracy by 2–3% over using either one alone.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The model learned to recognise crops from key stages of their growth cycle.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;It worked well in new regions with little training data, and it outperformed existing benchmark maps.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;We built 10-day composite images from Sentinel-1 radar and Sentinel-2 optical satellites. We used them to classify five crops (winter wheat, rapeseed, winter barley, silage maize and sugar beet) in Lower Saxony, North Rhine-Westphalia and Brandenburg for 2021 and 2023. We compared machine learning models (XGBoost, CatBoost) with deep learning models (BiLSTM, self-attention).&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;Because the method runs on Google Earth Engine, it can be scaled up to field-level crop monitoring across Europe. This supports farm decisions and long-term environmental monitoring.&lt;/p&gt;







