      Propagation patterns of different degree meteorological droughts across the Yangtze River Basin: a three-dimensional drought feature identification approach with Copula modeling

        Journal of Hydrology
         · Xing, L., Zhao, R., Sun, H., Tan, Z., Fang, Q., Li, M., Halder, K., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Droughts are not just a point on a map: they start somewhere, grow, move across a region and fade over time. This study tracks how meteorological droughts (periods of unusually low rainfall) of different severities travel across the Yangtze River Basin, one of China's most important river systems for food and water.&lt;/p&gt;

          &lt;p&gt;Drought events are identified in three dimensions (space plus time), so each event's area, duration and severity can be followed as it develops. Copula models, a statistical tool for linking several related variables, are used to describe how these characteristics occur together and how likely droughts of different severities are.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;Knowing how droughts of different severities typically spread helps water managers anticipate where a developing drought is heading and plan responses earlier.&lt;/p&gt;







