      High-Resolution Maize Yield Mapping across Africa using Earth Observation and Machine Learning, Deep Learning, and Foundation Model

        Science of Remote Sensing
         · Halder, K., Ewert, F., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Africa's food security is under pressure from climate change and population growth. Yet in much of the continent, farm yields are rarely measured. We produced maize yield maps at 250-metre resolution for 42 African countries.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;XGBoost was best at tracking changes from year to year (R² = 0.78).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;TabPFN, a pre-trained "foundation" AI model for tables of data, worked best in regions it had not seen and had the lowest error (about 25%).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Vegetation greenness, drought indicators and soil properties were the most important predictors.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The maps matched official national yields closely (R² &gt; 0.75).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;National FAO yield statistics were spread down to fine-scale pixels using satellite estimates of plant growth (net primary production). This created training data where no field measurements exist. We used 296 variables from Earth observation, climate and soil data, and tested XGBoost, LightGBM, a hybrid deep neural network and TabPFN. Each model was trained on past years and tested on later ones.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;This is the first use of a tabular foundation model for crop prediction across a whole continent. It offers a scalable way to monitor yields where ground data are scarce.&lt;/p&gt;







