      Improving landslide susceptibility prediction through ensemble Recursive Feature Elimination and meta-learning framework

        Scientific Reports
         · Halder, K., Srivastava, A. K., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Landslides threaten lives, homes and ecosystems in the fragile Sub-Himalayan region of West Bengal, India. We mapped where landslides are most likely to happen.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;A meta-classifier, which combines several models, performed best (95.6% accuracy, AUC 0.987).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Gradient Boosting, XGBoost and Random Forest also did very well, and needed fewer input variables.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The highest-risk zones are concentrated in Darjeeling and Kalimpong. Heavy rainfall, unstable geology, deforestation and urban growth drive the risk there.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;We used remote sensing and GIS to gather factors that make slopes unstable. Recursive Feature Elimination kept the most useful ones. We then trained seven machine learning models and stacked them together, and sorted the region into five susceptibility zones, from very low to very high.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;The maps can guide land-use planning, disaster preparedness and conservation. The method can be adapted to other landslide-prone regions.&lt;/p&gt;







