      Dynamic Modelling of Mixed Crop-Livestock Systems: A Case Study of Climate Change Impacts in sub-Saharan Africa

        Scientific Reports
         · Srivastava, A. K., Rahimi, J., Alsafadi, K., Vianna, M., Enders, A., Zheng, W., Demircan, A., Dieng, M. D. B., Salack, S., Faye, B., Singh, M., Halder, K., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Many smallholder farmers in sub-Saharan Africa grow crops and raise livestock together, and climate change threatens both. We extended the SIMPLACE modelling framework to simulate crops, grasslands and livestock together across 786,500 km² of West Africa.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;The model reproduced past conditions (1981–2005) well.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;By 2020–2050, the drier north could see crop and grass biomass fall by up to 56–57% and livestock numbers by up to 43%.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;In the Sahel this would mean about 41% less milk and 47% less meat, as well as lower methane emissions.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Some parts of the wetter Sudanian zone could instead see livestock numbers and methane rise by about 24%.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;We linked models of crops (maize, millet, sorghum), natural vegetation and livestock within SIMPLACE. We then ran them with regional climate projections, downscaled from global climate models, to estimate future yields, herd sizes and greenhouse gas emissions.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;Climate impacts on these farming systems vary widely from place to place, so adaptation plans need to be tailored to each region rather than one-size-fits-all.&lt;/p&gt;







    2024

      SAR-driven flood inventory and multi-factor ensemble susceptibility modelling using Machine Learning Frameworks

        Geomatics, Natural Hazards and Risk
         · Halder, K., Ghosh, A., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Floods are becoming more frequent and severe in the Indian subcontinent. We mapped which areas are most likely to flood so that planners can prepare.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;A stacking ensemble, which combines several models, gave the best predictions (0.965).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;It was followed by XGBoost (0.934), a deep neural network (0.929), Random Forest (0.925), Logistic Regression (0.921) and SVM (0.920).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;Past flood extents were mapped from Sentinel-1 radar images in Google Earth Engine, because radar can see through clouds. These records were used to train six machine learning models on 18 factors that influence flooding.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;The flood susceptibility maps give city planners and disaster managers practical guidance on where to strengthen infrastructure and community resilience.&lt;/p&gt;







    2024

      Application of bagging and boosting ensemble machine learning techniques for groundwater potential mapping in a drought-prone agriculture region of eastern India

        Environmental Sciences Europe
         · Halder, K., Srivastava, A. K., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Groundwater supplies drinking water and irrigation for billions of people, but over-pumping is depleting it. We mapped where groundwater is most likely to be found in Bankura, a drought-prone farming district in West Bengal, India.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;XGBoost and a voting ensemble, which combines models, performed best (about 96% accuracy). Random Forest and AdaBoost were close behind (about 94%).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;About 42% of the district has very low groundwater potential, and about 24% has very high potential.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The prediction uncertainty map shows where field checks are most needed.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;We trained four machine learning methods (Random Forest, AdaBoost, XGBoost and a voting ensemble) on locations where groundwater was known to be present or absent. They learned which environmental factors go with groundwater availability.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;The maps can help target well drilling, water conservation and recharge efforts. The approach can be used in other regions with similar geology.&lt;/p&gt;








