      Application of bagging and boosting ensemble machine learning techniques for groundwater potential mapping in a drought-prone agriculture region of eastern India

        Environmental Sciences Europe
         · Halder, K., Srivastava, A. K., et al.


        DOI




        Plain-language summary




          &lt;p&gt;Groundwater supplies drinking water and irrigation for billions of people, but over-pumping is depleting it. We mapped where groundwater is most likely to be found in Bankura, a drought-prone farming district in West Bengal, India.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;XGBoost and a voting ensemble, which combines models, performed best (about 96% accuracy). Random Forest and AdaBoost were close behind (about 94%).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;About 42% of the district has very low groundwater potential, and about 24% has very high potential.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;The prediction uncertainty map shows where field checks are most needed.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;We trained four machine learning methods (Random Forest, AdaBoost, XGBoost and a voting ensemble) on locations where groundwater was known to be present or absent. They learned which environmental factors go with groundwater availability.&lt;/p&gt;
          &lt;p&gt;The maps can help target well drilling, water conservation and recharge efforts. The approach can be used in other regions with similar geology.&lt;/p&gt;








